<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 750–945 (from the Abbasid Revolution to the Buyid takeover of Baghdad)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Caliph to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="House of Wisdom"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a><span class="soc-credit">Zereshk · Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">Abbasid society ranked people by religion and legal status first, then by descent, office and wealth. Islamic law divided the free into Muslims and protected non-Muslims (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>), who paid a poll tax, and set both above the enslaved. Among Muslims, descent from the Prophet’s clan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a>, gave the highest honour, while the old privilege of Arab birth faded as Persian and other converts (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a>) rose into the elite. Power lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>’s servants: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> and secretaries who ran the treasury and, from the 830s, Turkish soldiers, most of them bought as slaves, who came to make and unmake caliphs. The scholars of religious law (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>) won an authority of their own. Below them lived merchants, craftsmen and the peasants whose land tax paid for it all, while slaves brought from beyond the frontiers filled households, harems and armies. Most caliphs were sons of enslaved concubines.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 750–945 (from the Abbasid Revolution to the Buyid takeover of Baghdad)</span><span><b>People</b> not known</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 10</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The caliph</span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:39%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The Hashimite family</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:54%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Viziers and secretaries</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-unk" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Soldiers</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:70%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-unk" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Scholars of religious law</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-unk" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Merchants and bankers</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:85%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-unk" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Muslim townspeople</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-unk" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Protected non-Muslims</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-most" href="#rank-9" style="--sw:100%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Peasants</span><span class="soc-bar-s">Most people (the rest)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-unk" href="#rank-10" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Enslaved people</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people; ranks no one has counted are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/harun-al-rashid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">The caliph <span class="soc-term">khalīfa, amīr al-muʾminīn</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The caliph was the ruler of the Muslim empire and its leader in prayer.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> was the successor of the Prophet as head of the Muslim community, its “commander of the faithful”, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> claimed the office through descent from the Prophet’s uncle al-Abbas. He appointed governors, judges and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, commanded the army and led prayers at the great feasts, and his name stood on the coinage and in the Friday sermon (khutba). His viziers ran the government while he lived amid elaborate ceremony in the palaces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, founded by al-Mansur in 762, and from 836 to 892 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> (r. 786–809) ruled at the height of the dynasty’s wealth; al-Ma’mun (r. 813–833) tried to impose his own theology on the religious scholars and failed. From 861 Turkish soldiers murdered or deposed four caliphs in nine years, and from 936 a military commander, the amir al-umara, held real power, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> took Baghdad in 945 and left the caliph a figurehead.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Only an Abbasid could be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, but no rule fixed the succession: caliphs named heirs among their sons and brothers, and the choice was often fought over, as in the civil war of 811–813 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> and al-Ma’mun. Most caliphs were sons of enslaved concubines.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, with his household and harem.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">2</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">The Hashimite family <span class="soc-term">Banū Hāshim, Hāshimī</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Families descended from the Prophet Muhammad’s clan were the most honoured in the empire.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a>, the clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, became the highest mark of nobility among Muslims. The Hashimites included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> themselves, descended from the Prophet’s uncle al-Abbas, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a>, descendants of his cousin and son-in-law Ali, whom the Shia held to be the rightful leaders of the community. Abbasid princes married within the family, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> married his cousin Zubayda, a granddaughter of al-Mansur, and they governed provinces: Harun’s succession plan of 802 gave al-Ma’mun Khurasan, and al-Mu’tasim governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> for his brother. They were also watched as rivals: al-Mu’tazz was imprisoned by his cousin al-Musta’in, and al-Ma’mun’s son al-Abbas was left to die of thirst after a plot in 838. The Alids were honoured and feared: al-Mansur crushed the revolt of the Alid al-Nafs al-Zakiyya in 762, while al-Ma’mun named the Alid Ali al-Rida his heir in 817 before abandoning the plan. A prince such as al-Muwaffaq, regent for his brother al-Mu’tamid, could hold the real power of the state.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Hashimite rank came only by birth in the male line. A prince could rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, or end in prison or worse at the hands of a rival.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count of the Abbasid and Alid families.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muwaffaq">Al-Muwaffaq</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">3</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Viziers and secretaries <span class="soc-term">wazīr, kātib (pl. kuttāb)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Viziers and secretaries ran the caliph’s government, kept its accounts and wrote its letters.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>’s government was run from offices (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwans</a>) for taxes, the army, correspondence and the post. They were staffed by secretaries (kuttab), skilled in accounts and in the intricate land tax, who saw themselves as the mainstay of the state and passed their knowledge from father to son. At their head stood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, and most viziers were of non-Arab origin. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakids</a>, an Iranian family descended from the Buddhist chiefs of a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, ran the state for much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>’s reign, until in 803 he imprisoned them and executed Ja’far. In the early 10th century two secretarial dynasties, the Banu al-Furat and the Banu al-Jarrah, the latter from converted Christian families, fought for the vizierate. Office brought great wealth but no security: a fallen vizier and his clients were fined and tortured to give up their fortunes, a practice called musadara. Ali ibn Isa, vizier from 913, was remembered as the “good vizier” for his honesty.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Secretaries rose by skill, family and patronage; many were converts or their sons, and Christians served as secretaries too. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> could raise a secretary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizierate</a> and ruin him as quickly.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count of the secretaries in the government offices.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">Vizier</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakids</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-4"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/samarra" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/samarra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Soldiers <span class="soc-term">jund, abnāʾ al-dawla, ghilmān</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Many of the caliph’s soldiers were Turks from Central Asia who fought on horseback with bows.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The army that won the revolution of 747–750 came from Khurasan, and its men and their sons, the abna al-dawla (“sons of the dynasty”), settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> as the regime’s privileged soldiers, paid by the state. They fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> in the civil war of 811–813 and lost. Al-Mu’tasim (r. 833–842) built a new army around Turks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, most of them bought or captured as slaves beyond the frontier, trained from boyhood as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>, paid regular salaries and probably freed; at his accession they numbered perhaps three to four thousand. Their quarrels with the people of Baghdad led him to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> in 836, with separate quarters for each regiment. Turkish commanders such as Ashinas, Itakh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugha-the-elder">Bugha the Elder</a> became the most powerful men in the state, and when pay failed their troops rioted: in 861–870 they murdered or deposed four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>. From the early 10th century soldiers were increasingly paid with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqtas</a>, the right to collect the taxes of a district.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A slave boy bought on the steppe could become a general: Itakh began as a cook, and Mu’nis, a Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> slave, commanded the whole army from 908 to 933. Commanders who lost a struggle at court were dismissed, imprisoned or killed.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No count of the empire’s people allows a share: al-Mu’tasim’s Turkish guard numbered perhaps three to four thousand at his accession in 833, and Michael the Syrian put his army against Amorium in 838 at 80,000.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahir-ibn-husayn">Tahir ibn Husayn</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afshin">Al-Afshin</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugha-the-elder">Bugha the Elder</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">Ghulam</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchy-at-samarra">Anarchy at Samarra</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">5</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Scholars of religious law <span class="soc-term">ʿulamāʾ, faqīh, qāḍī</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">The ulama were Muslim scholars who studied the Qur’an and religious law, and some were judges.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> were the scholars of the Qur’an, the traditions of the Prophet (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>) and religious law. They had no ordination or hierarchy: a student sought out renowned teachers and received their licence to teach. In these two centuries they built the schools of law named after Abu Hanifa, Malik, al-Shafi’i and Ahmad ibn Hanbal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> appointed and dismissed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a>, judges of religious law, in the cities, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> made Abu Yusuf, a pupil of Abu Hanifa, the first chief qadi; his Book of the Land Tax (Kitab al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">Kharaj</a>) set out the rules of taxation. In 833 al-Ma’mun began the Mihna, an inquisition that required judges and scholars to profess that the Qur’an was created, and punished those who refused. Ahmad ibn Hanbal refused, was imprisoned and flogged, and became a hero to the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The Mihna was abandoned around 850, and its failure left the ulama, rather than the caliph, as the recognised guardians of religious law.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Learning was open to any Muslim who could find teachers, converts and their sons included; a qadi, though, held office only at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>’s pleasure.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count of the scholars and judges.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutazilism">Mu'tazilism</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-6"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">6</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Merchants and bankers <span class="soc-term">tājir (pl. tujjār), jahbadh</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Abbasid merchants sailed as far as China and brought back silk and fine pottery.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Trade carried honour in a faith whose Prophet had been a merchant, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> joined the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. Ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraf">Siraf</a> sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and a whole section of the market of east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> sold Chinese goods, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>; caravans brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, gold and slaves, and Jewish merchants known as the Radhanites linked the Muslim world with Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Merchants were Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> alike. Bankers lent to the government, and in the late 9th and early 10th centuries Jewish financiers were important at the caliph’s court. Voyages were dangerous, for ships sank and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> preyed on the sea routes, and it was said that anyone who reached China and came back unharmed was blessed by God. Wealth also drew the powerful: in the troubles of the later 9th century soldiers extorted money from anyone who seemed rich.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Trade was open to any free man with capital, Muslim or not, and a great financier could win the ear of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a>; but a lost ship or an extortion could ruin a house.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count of the merchant families.</p></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">7</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Muslim townspeople <span class="soc-term">ʿāmma, mawālī</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Many townspeople were craftsmen and shopkeepers who worked in busy markets.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The free Muslims of the towns, craftsmen, shopkeepers, porters, water-carriers and labourers, filled the markets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and the provincial capitals; Baghdad, founded in 762, was probably the largest city in the world until the 930s. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, non-Arab converts had to attach themselves as clients (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a>) to Arab tribes and were treated as lesser Muslims. Their grievances helped bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> to power, and under the new dynasty the distinction faded, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, Turks and others entered the elite and Arabic became the everyday language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. The Shu’ubiyya, a literary movement of the early Abbasid period, argued that non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> were the equals of the Arabs, or their betters. Townspeople paid the alms tax (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a>) rather than the poll tax, and they could make themselves heard: crowds in Baghdad rioted against the Turkish soldiers in 836, and took the side of Ahmad ibn Hanbal in the Mihna.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Converts joined this rank freely, since conversion lifted the poll tax, and a townsman could rise by trade, by learning or by service in the army or a government office.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: Baghdad alone may have held hundreds of thousands of people, on some estimates over a million, and a modern model puts some 1.8 million of Iraq’s roughly 5.8 million people around 800 outside farming.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">Mawali</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-8"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">8</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Protected non-Muslims <span class="soc-term">ahl al-dhimma, dhimmī</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Christians, Jews and Zoroastrians kept their own religions but paid a special tax.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Christians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>, protected peoples. They kept their faith, their places of worship and their own leaders and courts, and Islamic law forbade enslaving them. In return they paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, a yearly poll tax on adult men graded by wealth; on one modern estimate most paid 12 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a>. The law also barred them from building new places of worship, bearing arms or riding horses, gave their testimony no weight against a Muslim, and let a Muslim man marry a Christian or Jewish woman but not the reverse. Enforcement depended on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>: al-Mutawakkil (r. 847–861) ordered dhimmis to wear distinguishing yellow garments and barred them from office, but after his reign such rules lapsed again. Dhimmis prospered in the professions. Eight generations of the Christian Bukhtishu family served the caliphs as physicians, the Christian Hunayn ibn Ishaq translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> into Arabic, and Christian secretaries and Jewish bankers served the government.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> lifted the poll tax and opened every career, and conversion spread steadily: Richard Bulliet estimated that Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> grew from about 40 per cent of the people in the mid-9th century to nearly 80 per cent by the end of the 11th.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No count for the whole caliphate: Richard Bulliet’s estimate that Muslims were only about 40 per cent of Iran’s people by the mid-9th century suggests that non-Muslims long remained a large part of the population. Non-Muslim villagers are counted with the peasants.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">Dhimmi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">Jizya</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-9"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">9</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>Most people (the rest)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Peasants <span class="soc-term">fallāḥ (pl. fallāḥūn)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Most people were farmers who grew wheat, barley and dates and paid heavy taxes on their harvests.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Most people lived by farming, in the irrigated plain of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawad">Sawad</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Muslims and non-Muslims alike. The land tax (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">kharaj</a>) on their fields paid for the court and the army; by Abbasid times it was due on most land whatever the owner’s faith. From the reign of al-Mahdi (775–785) the Sawad paid with a share of the crop: half on land watered by flooding, a third or a quarter where water had to be raised by wheels. Many cultivators were tenants on estates whose owners lived in the towns, and a modern model suggests that after taxes and rents they kept little more than a third of what they grew. Heavy taxes and the disorders of the later 9th century drove many from the land, and the Sawad’s revenue fell from about 100 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> to 20 million by the early 10th century.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A peasant who converted escaped the poll tax but not the land tax. Many fled to the towns; others revolted, like the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> in 831–832, after which many of their women and children were sold as slaves.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other tiers: the great majority, cultivators of every faith (the model of Robert Allen and Leander Heldring puts the farming population of Iraq around 800 at some 4 million of about 5.8 million). This share also takes in the Hashimites, secretaries, soldiers, scholars, merchants, townspeople, dhimmis and slaves, whose numbers are unknown.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">Kharaj</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-10"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/baqt" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/baqt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Enslaved people <span class="soc-term">ʿabd, jāriya, khādim</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; some were later freed.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Islamic law forbade enslaving Muslims or protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>, so slaves came from beyond the frontiers: war captives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, Turks from the Central Asian steppe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and other Europeans brought down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, and Africans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and the East African coast. Under the Baqt treaty Nubian Makuria sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> between 360 and 400 slaves a year. Many slaves served in households, and freeing a slave was counted a pious act. Enslaved women worked as servants or were bought as concubines; a concubine who bore her master a child he acknowledged became an umm walad, could no longer be sold and went free at his death, and her child was born free. Trained singing slaves (qiyan) were the most expensive of all. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">Eunuchs</a> guarded the harems and served as stewards and officials. In southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> enslaved Africans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a>, were kept in labour camps to scrape the salty topsoil from the marshes near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>; in 869 they rose under Ali ibn Muhammad and held out for fourteen years.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freeing a slave was counted a pious act, and a slave could also buy freedom by contract. Freed slaves became clients (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a>) of their former owners, and many rose high: the mothers of most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> had been slaves.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: slaves were imported in large numbers from Africa, the steppe and Europe, but never counted.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion">Zanj Rebellion</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">Eunuch</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baqt">Baqt</a></div></div></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="abbasid-caliphate">Be born in the Abbasid Caliphate</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>Abbasid society was steep, but its upper ranks were unusually open to outsiders, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> trusted men who owed everything to them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a>, a Persian freedman of the Abbasid family, led the revolution of 747–750. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakids</a>, descended from the Buddhist chiefs of a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, rose to run the state for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> before their fall in 803. Slaves rose highest of all. Al-Khayzuran, sold in a slave market near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, became the wife of al-Mahdi and the mother of two caliphs; Turkish slave boys became the generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>; Mu’nis, a Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> slave, commanded the army from 908 and in 928 briefly deposed his caliph. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ahmad ibn Tulun</a>, a Turkish soldier sent to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 868, made himself its master, and Ya’qub ibn al-Layth, a coppersmith, carved out a kingdom in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Converts entered every career. Falls were sudden: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> and their followers were fined and tortured, and caliphs themselves were deposed, starved or murdered.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>Islamic law let a free woman own property, gave her a dowry from her husband and a share of inheritance, half that of a brother, but in this period women of the upper and middle classes were secluded in the harem as never before: after al-Khayzuran’s time the women of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>’s family vanished from public life. Seclusion did not mean powerlessness. Al-Khayzuran, a former slave, received officials and petitioners and grew rich in trade while her husband al-Mahdi and her son Harun ruled; Harun’s wife Zubayda paid for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> along the pilgrim road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, the Darb Zubayda; Shaghab, mother of al-Muqtadir, held great influence at court during his reign (908–932). The princess Ulayya bint al-Mahdi was a celebrated poet and singer. Enslaved women had no such protection: concubines and singing slaves (qiyan) performed unveiled before men, though Arib al-Ma’muniyya, a slave singer freed by al-Mu’tasim, became a landowner with a troupe of her own.</p></section>
