ny of their women and children were sold as slaves.","why":"Most people lived by farming, in the irrigated plain of southern Iraq, the Sawad, in the Nile valley, Iran and Syria, Muslims and non-Muslims alike.","people":[]},{"n":10,"lv":6,"band":"enslaved","name":"Enslaved people","term":"ʿabd, jāriya, khādim","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/baqt.jpg","you":"You were captured or bought beyond the frontier and now serve in a Baghdad household; your owner may free you as a pious act, and if you are a woman who bears him a child he acknowledges, you cannot be sold and will go free at his death.","kid":"Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; some were later freed.","rise":"Freeing a slave was counted a pious act, and a slave could also buy freedom by contract. Freed slaves became clients (mawali) of their former owners, and many rose high: the mothers of most caliphs had been slaves.","why":"Islamic law forbade enslaving Muslims or protected dhimmis, so slaves came from beyond the frontiers: war captives from Byzantium, Turks from the Central Asian steppe, Slavs and other Europeans brought down the Volga…","people":[]}]}</script>
